I'll start by checking if K is 1. If it is, I'll sort the string and return it. If K is greater than 1, I'll sort the string and move the first K letters to the end. I'll then check if the resulting string is smaller than the original string. If it is, I'll return the resulting string. If it isn't, I'll return the original string.

Here's my solution:

```python
def orderly_queue(S, K):
    if K == 1:
        return ''.join(sorted(S))
    else:
        sorted_S = ''.join(sorted(S))
        for i in range(len(S)):
            if sorted_S[i:] + sorted_S[:i] < S:
                return sorted_S[i:] + sorted_S[:i]
        return S
```

This solution has a time complexity of O(n log n) due to the sorting operation and a space complexity of O(n) due to the creation of the sorted string.
</reasoning>
