First, we'll iterate over the matrix. For each 'X' we encounter, we'll check if it's part of a battleship. If it is, we'll mark all the cells in that battleship as 'V' (visited), to prevent counting the same battleship more than once. We'll also increment our battleship count.

To check if a cell is part of a battleship, we need to check its neighbors. If any neighbor is also an 'X', then the cell is part of a battleship.

The follow-up asks for one-pass, O(1) extra memory. This means we can't use a separate matrix to mark visited cells. Instead, we'll use the same matrix to mark visited cells, but only when we've seen an 'X' that's not part of a battleship. We'll need to be careful not to mark cells that are part of a battleship as visited prematurely.

This solution ensures that we count each battleship only once and that we don't modify the board in any way.

