2025-04-25 00:46:01 - DEBUG - __main__ - Parsing failed: Could not find tag 'solution' in output snippet: <reasoning>First, we need to understand the problem. We have an array of stock prices, and we want to find the maximum profit we can make by buying and selling the stock at most k times. This is a variation of the classic "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock" problem, but with an added constraint.

My approach will be to use dynamic programming. We'll create a 2D array dp where dp[i][j] represents the maximum profit we can make if we have made j transactions up to the i-th day.

The base case is when j = 0, which means we haven't made any transactions yet. In this case, the maximum profit is 0.

For j > 0, we have two choices on the i-th day: either we buy the stock (which means we have to sell it on some previous day to make a profit) or we don't buy it. If we buy it, our profit is the maximum of our current profit (if we didn't buy it before) plus the difference between the price of the stock on the i-th day and the price of the stock on some previous day (if we sold it before). If we don't buy it, our profit is the same as our current profit. We take the maximum of these two options.

Finally, the maximum profit we can make is dp[n][k], where n is the number of days in the array.

Here's my solution:

<code>
def max_profit(prices, k):
    n = len(prices)
    dp = [[0 for _ in range(k+1)] for _ in range(n)]
    for j in range(1, k+1):
        max_diff = -prices[0]
        for i in range(1, n):
            dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], prices[i] + max_diff)
            max_diff = max(max_diff, dp[i-1][j-1] - prices[i])
    return dp[n-1][k]
</code>

This solution has a time complexity of O(nk) and a space complexity of O(nk).

