The base case for our recursive function is when we reach the root node (node 1). In this case, we return 1, since the cost of the path from the root to itself is 1.

For any other node, we first calculate the minimum value among all the nodes to its left in the path sequence, including itself. This is done by recursively calling the function on the parent node and passing in the current node’s value.

We then add this minimum value to the cost of the path from the parent node to the root, and return this sum as the cost of the path from the current node to the root.

This recursive approach ensures that we calculate the minimum value on each path and sum up the costs of all the nodes in the path. We can then return the final cost of the path from each node to the root.
