To solve this, I'll use dynamic programming. We'll maintain a 2D array `dp` where `dp[i][j]` represents the minimum number of days required to make the world corona-free if we distribute `j` cures to country `i` on day `i`. We'll initialize `dp[0][0] = 0` and all other entries to infinity.

Now, for each country `i` and number of cures `j`, we'll check if `j` is less than or equal to the population of country `i`. If it is, we can distribute `j` cures to country `i` on day `i` and update the number of infected people in country `i` by doubling it. Then, we'll calculate the minimum number of days required to make the world corona-free after distributing `j` cures to country `i` and update `dp[i][j]` with the minimum of its current value and the new minimum.

After iterating through all countries and cures, we'll return `dp[N][x]`, which represents the minimum number of days required to make the world corona-free if we distribute `x` cures to all countries.

However, since the number of cures can double each day, we need to consider that as well. To do this, we'll iterate through each day and calculate the number of cures needed to make the world corona-free if we distribute `x` cures to all countries on that day. We'll update the minimum number of days required to make the world corona-free with the minimum of its current value and the new minimum.

