Ash has N pokemon, each of them has a unique ID from 1 to N. Ash wants to catch them all. But, Ash doesn't know which pokemon will appear next. Ash knows that the probability of catching any particular pokemon is equal. He wants to find the expected number of attempts he needs to catch all the pokemon. Can you help Ash?

-----Input:-----
The first line of input contains a single integer T denoting the number of test cases.
The first line of each test case contains a single integer N denoting the number of pokemon.

-----Output:-----
For each test case, print a single line containing one integer - the expected number of attempts Ash needs to catch all the pokemon.

-----Constraints-----
-
1 <= T <= 103
-
1 <= N <= 105

-----Subtasks-----
Subtask #1 (30 points):
- N <= 100

-----Sample Input:-----
2

2

3

-----Sample Output:-----
2

4

-----EXPLANATION:-----
Example Case 1:

There are 2 pokemon. The probability of catching either one is 1/2. So, the expected number of attempts is 2.
Example Case 2:

There are 3 pokemon. The probability of catching any one of them is 1/3. So, the expected number of attempts is 4.
