To implement this, we'll first sort the rectangles by their x-coordinates. Then, we'll iterate over each x-coordinate, updating the current area as we go. We'll keep track of the current y-coordinate for each rectangle and its height, and use a priority queue (min heap) to keep track of the rectangles that intersect the current x-coordinate. This way, we can efficiently compute the current area covered by the rectangles at each x-coordinate.

We'll also need to handle the case where two rectangles intersect at the same point. In this case, we'll add the height of both rectangles to the current area, but we'll subtract the height of the smaller rectangle to account for the overlap.
