First, I need to parse the input string into a list of integers. Then, for each integer, I'll set the corresponding bit in the bitmap to `1`. Finally, I'll convert the bitmap into an array of `1`s and `0`s.

To make sure the bitmap is correctly sized, I'll initialize it with `5000` zeros. Then, I'll iterate through each integer in the list and set the corresponding bit to `1`.
</reasoning>

<solution>
```javascript
function toBits(inputString) {
    // Parse the input string into a list of integers
    const numbers = inputString.split('\n').map(Number);

    // Initialize a bitmap of 5000 bits, all set to 0
    const bitmap = new Array(5000).fill(0);

    // Set the corresponding bit for each number in the list
    for (let number of numbers) {
        bitmap[number] = 1;
    }

    // Convert the bitmap to an array of 1s and 0s
    const bitmapArray = bitmap.map(bit => bit.toString());

    return bitmapArray;
}

// Example usage
let exampleInput = '3\n14\n5\n19\n18\n1\n8\n11\n2';
let exampleOutput = toBits(exampleInput);
console.log(exampleOutput);
```

This code does exactly what's requested. It takes an input string of space-separated integers, parses them into an array, then creates a bitmap of 5000 bits where each bit corresponds to an integer in the array. It finally converts this bitmap into an array of `1`s and `0`s and returns it. Happy coding!...
