The solution will involve a loop to generate numbers within the range, checking each number against the constraints. If a number meets all criteria, it’s added to the list of selected numbers.

<code>
```python
def sel_number(n, d):
    # Initialize the list of selected numbers
    selected_numbers = []

    # Loop through numbers from 1 to n
    for num in range(1, n+1):
        # Convert number to string to easily access its digits
        num_str = str(num)

        # Check if the number has at least two digits
        if len(num_str) < 2:
            continue

        # Initialize flag for no repeated digits
        has_no_repeats = True

        # Check for no repeated digits
        for i in range(len(num_str) - 1):
            if num_str[i] == num_str[i+1]:
                has_no_repeats = False
                break

        # If no repeated digits, check digit order and difference
        if has_no_repeats:
            # Check digit order
            if all(int(num_str[i]) < int(num_str[i+1]) for i in range(len(num_str) - 1)):
                # Check digit difference
                if all(abs(int(num_str[i]) - int(num_str[i+1])) <= d for i in range(len(num_str) - 1)):
                    # If all checks pass, add to selected numbers
                    selected_numbers.append(num)

    # Return the list of selected numbers
    return selected_numbers
```
</code>

This solution is structured to follow the problem’s constraints, ensuring each number is evaluated correctly. The function is clean, readable, and adheres to best practices in Python programming. Happy coding!...
