def mincostTickets(days, costs):
    n = len(days)
    dp = [0] * (n+1)

    for i in range(1, n+1):
        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + costs[0] # cost of traveling on current day

        # check if we can use a 7-day pass
        j = i-1
        while j >= 0 and days[j] >= days[i-1]-6:
            j -= 1
        dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[j+1] + costs[1])

        # check if we can use a 30-day pass
        j = i-1
        while j >= 0 and days[j] >= days[i-1]-29:
            j -= 1
        dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[j+1] + costs[2])

    return dp[n]
```
</code>

This solution has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n). It uses dynamic programming to find the minimum cost to travel every day on the given list of days. It iterates through the days and calculates the cost of traveling on each day, comparing it to the minimum cost of traveling up to the previous day. It also checks if we can use a 7-day or 30-day pass and updates the minimum cost accordingly. Finally, it returns the minimum cost to travel every day on the given list of days.
