To solve this problem, we can use a priority queue to keep track of the flasks with the lowest concentration. We can then repeatedly merge pairs of flasks and update the priority queue until we have a single flask. The final concentration will be the concentration of the final flask.

<solution>
```python
import heapq

def min_concentration(flasks):
    # Create a priority queue and add all flasks to it
    pq = [(-a, i) for i, a in enumerate(flasks)]
    heapq.heapify(pq)

    # Merge pairs of flasks until we have a single flask
    while len(pq) > 1:
        # Merge the two flasks with the lowest concentration
        a, i = heapq.heappop(pq)
        b, j = heapq.heappop(pq)
        heapq.heappush(pq, (-(a + b), len(flasks)))
        flasks.append(a + b)

    # Return the concentration of the final flask
    return -pq[0][0] / len(flasks)
```
